  importantPoints: ["1. Need for Political Institutions: Institutions provide established procedures for making decisions, implementing policies and resolving disputes. They make government more predictable and distribute responsibilities among different authorities.", "2. Parliament: Parliament is a central legislative institution. It discusses public issues, makes laws, controls public finance and holds the executive accountable through questions, debates and other procedures. The Lok Sabha and Rajya Sabha form the two Houses of India's Parliament.", "3. Political Executive: The political executive consists of elected or politically accountable leaders who take major policy decisions. In the parliamentary system, the Council of Ministers headed by the Prime Minister exercises executive authority while remaining responsible to the legislature.", "4. Prime Minister and Council of Ministers: The Prime Minister leads the Council of Ministers, coordinates government work and plays a central role in advising the President. Ministers head departments and participate in collective decision-making. The Council of Ministers is collectively responsible to the Lok Sabha.", "5. President: The President is the constitutional head of the Union executive. The office performs important constitutional functions, but in the parliamentary system most executive decisions are taken on the aid and advice of the Council of Ministers.", "6. Permanent Executive: Civil servants provide continuity and professional administration. They implement policies and laws, maintain records and provide specialized expertise. Their role differs from that of political executives, who are accountable for political choices and can change with governments.", "7. Judiciary: The judiciary interprets laws, resolves disputes and protects constitutional principles. An independent judiciary can review government action and provide remedies when legal or constitutional rights are violated.", "8. Checks and Balances: Institutions are designed so that no single authority should exercise unlimited power. Legislative oversight, judicial review, constitutional limits and political accountability create multiple forms of restraint.", "9. Decision-Making in Practice: A public policy may involve political priorities, cabinet decisions, legislative approval, administrative implementation and judicial scrutiny. Understanding the chain prevents the misconception that one individual can simply command the entire state.", "10. Institutional Accountability: Institutions are effective when procedures are followed, records are available, decisions can be questioned and unlawful actions can be challenged. Democratic institutions depend on both formal rules and political practice."],
  chapterSummary: "Modern democratic government operates through institutions rather than through individual rulers alone. Parliament, the political executive, civil services and the judiciary perform different roles. Institutional procedures help turn public decisions into laws and policies while creating accountability and checks on power. 1. Need for Political Institutions — Institutions provide established procedures for making decisions, implementing policies and resolving disputes. They make government more predictable and distribute responsibilities among different authorities. 2. Parliament — Parliament is a central legislative institution. It discusses public issues, makes laws, controls public finance and holds the executive accountable through questions, debates and other procedures. The Lok Sabha and Rajya Sabha form the two Houses of India's Parliament. 3. Political Executive — The political executive consists of elected or politically accountable leaders who take major policy decisions. In the parliamentary system, the Council of Ministers headed by the Prime Minister exercises executive authority while remaining responsible to the legislature. 4. Prime Minister and Council of Ministers — The Prime Minister leads the Council of Ministers, coordinates government work and plays a central role in advising the President. Ministers head departments and participate in collective decision-making. The Council of Ministers is collectively responsible to the Lok Sabha. 5. President — The President is the constitutional head of the Union executive. The office performs important constitutional functions, but in the parliamentary system most executive decisions are taken on the aid and advice of the Council of Ministers. 6. Permanent Executive — Civil servants provide continuity and professional administration. They implement policies and laws, maintain records and provide specialized expertise. Their role differs from that of political executives, who are accountable for political choices and can change with governments. 7. Judiciary — The judiciary interprets laws, resolves disputes and protects constitutional principles. An independent judiciary can review government action and provide remedies when legal or constitutional rights are violated. 8. Checks and Balances — Institutions are designed so that no single authority should exercise unlimited power. Legislative oversight, judicial review, constitutional limits and political accountability create multiple forms of restraint. 9. Decision-Making in Practice — A public policy may involve political priorities, cabinet decisions, legislative approval, administrative implementation and judicial scrutiny. Understanding the chain prevents the misconception that one individual can simply command the entire state. 10. Institutional Accountability — Institutions are effective when procedures are followed, records are available, decisions can be questioned and unlawful actions can be challenged. Democratic institutions depend on both formal rules and political practice.",
